-<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/buyers/new-construction/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need a realtor to buy new construction?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"You are allowed to buy without one, but the sales agent in the model home represents the builder. Your own agent reviews the contract, the lot, the incentives, and the inspection findings for your side. In most Grand Strand communities the builder pays the buyer agent fee from its sales budget, and we confirm in writing what, if anything, you would pay before you tour."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does using my own agent make a new build cost more?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Builders almost never discount the price for unrepresented buyers, because doing so would undercut the comps in their own community. Since August 2024, representation terms are set in writing up front, so you know the cost before you tour, and in most local communities the builder covers it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I have to use the builder's lender?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Incentives are often tied to the builder's preferred lender, so compare complete loan estimates side by side. A credit at closing does not automatically beat a better loan."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Should I get an inspection on a new build?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and ideally three: before drywall goes up, before closing, and again around month eleven, before the one-year workmanship warranty runs out."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What warranty comes with a new home in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"South Carolina has no statutory new-home warranty. Most builders offer a written warranty, commonly one year on workmanship, two on systems, and ten on structure, plus the implied warranties South Carolina courts recognize. Read it before you sign, not after."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Are builder incentives worth it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Sometimes. Price the incentive against the rest of the purchase: the lot premium, the upgrade pricing, the loan terms behind it, and what resales in the community sell for."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What should I know about construction defects?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Defect complaints have been rising in South Carolina, including the coast, with water intrusion the most common issue near the beach. South Carolina generally allows three years from discovery to bring a claim, with an eight-year outer limit. That is why the eleventh-month inspection matters."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Should I buy a spec home or to-be-built?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A spec home is finished or nearly finished, closes fast, and you can walk the finished house before you buy it. A to-be-built gives you the lot and finishes but takes months and carries timeline risk. Pricing and incentives differ between the two, and quarter-end spec homes often carry the best deals."}}]}</script>
