-<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/hoa/hoa-vs-poa/#article","headline":"HOA vs POA vs Condo Regime in South Carolina","description":"What separates an HOA, a POA and a South Carolina condo regime: what you own, who maintains and insures what, and why regime fees are not comparable to HOA dues.","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/hoa/hoa-vs-poa/","author":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/about/#devin-day"},"reviewedBy":{"@type":"Person","name":"Timothy Nash","alternateName":"Tim Nash","jobTitle":"Broker-in-Charge"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/#org"},"datePublished":"2026-07-27","dateModified":"2026-10-28","inLanguage":"en-US","image":"https://chapter3realty.com/og-image.jpg","mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/hoa/hoa-vs-poa/#webpage"},"about":{"@type":"Thing","name":"HOA vs POA vs Condo Regime in South Carolina"},"keywords":"HOA vs POA South Carolina, regime fee South Carolina, condo regime vs HOA"}</script>
-<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/hoa/hoa-vs-poa/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between an HOA and a POA?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The terms are used loosely and both describe an association of owners funded by assessments. What matters is which documents govern your property, what you own, and what the association is responsible for. Read the declaration, not the name."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a regime fee in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Regime fee is the common local term for the assessment paid by owners in a horizontal property regime, which is what South Carolina calls a condominium. Functionally it is condo dues."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What do I own in a condo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Your unit, plus an undivided interest in the common elements shared with every other owner. That is different from a single-family lot in an HOA community, where you own the lot and the structure and the association maintains shared areas."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who insures what in a condo versus a single-family HOA?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In a condo regime the association carries a master policy on the building and owners carry an individual policy for the interior and their belongings. In a single-family HOA you insure your own home and the association insures the common property. Our coastal insurance page covers how the pieces stack."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can a community have more than one association?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Master-planned communities often have a master association plus sub-associations for individual neighborhoods or buildings, which means two sets of documents and sometimes two assessments. Check for both."}}]}</script>
