ion{color:var(--muted);font-size:.95rem;line-height:1.55;margin:1.1rem 0 0;padding:0}@media (max-width:699px){.c3-gchart{padding:1rem .95rem .9rem}.c3-gchart .b{width:calc((100% - 6.2rem) * var(--f))}}</style><style>.c3-glance{list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0;display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(var(--n),minmax(0,1fr));gap:.75rem;max-width:760px}.c3-glance li{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.55rem;margin:0;padding:.5rem .6rem;background:rgba(255,255,255,.55);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:6px;line-height:1.25}.c3-glance .i{flex:0 0 auto;display:flex;width:2rem;height:2rem;border-radius:50%;background:var(--ivory-2);color:var(--brass-ink);align-items:center;justify-content:center}.c3-glance strong{display:block;color:var(--navy);font-size:.9rem;font-weight:500}.c3-glance span.t{display:block;color:var(--muted);font-size:1rem}@media (max-width:699px){.c3-glance{gap:.3rem}.c3-glance li{flex-direction:column;justify-content:flex-start;text-align:center;gap:.2rem;padding:.4rem .1rem}.c3-glance .i{width:1.6rem;height:1.6rem}.c3-glance strong{font-size:.875rem}.c3-glance span.t{font-size:1rem;line-height:1.2}}</style><ul role="list" class="c3-glance" style="--n:3"><li><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 11.5 12 3.5l9 8M5.5 10v10.5h13V10M10 20.5V15h4v5.5"/></svg></span><span><strong>14 of 2,975</strong><span class="t">houses for sale pass the 1% rule</span></span></li><li><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 3h8l4 4v13a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H6a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V4a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM14 3v4h4M9 17l6-6"/><circle cx="9.5" cy="11.5" r="1"/><circle cx="14.5" cy="16.5" r="1"/></svg></span><span><strong>About $1,200</strong><span class="t">tax a year per $100,000 on a rental</span></span></li><li><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M15 9.4c-.4-1-1.5-1.8-3-1.8-1.8 0-3 1-3 2.2 0 3 6 1.6 6 4.4 0 1.3-1.3 2.2-3 2.2-1.6 0-2.7-.7-3.1-1.8M12 5.8v1.8M12 16.4v1.8"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/></svg></span><span><strong>23 to 48%</strong><span class="t">of a 2-bedroom condo's rent goes to dues</span></span></li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 4, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">A rental analyzer you can run on any Grand Strand address, the tax a rental pays in each town, and what condo dues do to the numbers.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Usually not, if you want cash flow: the rent left after costs. Take out tax, HOA dues, management and repairs. The middle house for sale in Carolina Forest or Longs keeps about 4 to 5 percent of its price, before insurance and any loan. A condo keeps about 1.4 percent. Our target is 6 percent. The rest is the rise in price, which beat our 3 percent target over five and ten years.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The tax is higher than the listing shows. A rental pays about $1,200 a year for each $100,000 of value outside city limits, about 3 times what an owner living there pays. The year after you buy, the county resets the value to about what you paid.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="calculator"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Analyzer">How do you run the numbers on a Myrtle Beach rental?</h2><style>
