-## Why Grand Strand homes keep appreciating.
+### Houses
 
-Typical home value, Myrtle Beach metro
+- All houses 3.8 months
 
-Estimated typical home values for the Myrtle Beach metro, drawn from the Federal Housing Finance Agency home price index and scaled to today's typical value near $310,000. Values dipped after 2008, drifted through the 2010s, then climbed steeply from 2021. The typical home has more than doubled since its 2012 to 2013 low. The dotted line projects values to 2030 if the long-run appreciation rate continues. It is an estimate, not a guarantee. Hover any year for its value.
+- Houses under $300K 3.3 months
 
-Source: FHFA All-Transactions House Price Index for the Myrtle Beach metro (public data), indexed to current market value. A typical, blended figure. Single-family homes, condos, and individual neighborhoods each vary.
+- Houses $300K to $450K 3.2 months
 
-+131% since the 2012 low
-+85% since 2019
+- Houses $450K to $700K 4.4 months
 
-Five forces push in the same direction, and they are structural rather than seasonal:
+- Houses $700K and up about 7 months
 
-In-migration. Myrtle Beach is the second fastest-growing metro area in the country (U.S. Census Bureau, latest estimates), and North Carolina is the top source of buyers.
+Homes for sale today against homes sold in the past year across Horry County, builders' sales included. Houses include townhouses and manufactured homes.
+Condos as a whole are past 7 months; two condo price ranges sit just under 7, at the top of balanced. On a condo there is room to ask for a lower price or for repairs. On a house under $700,000 there is less room: those houses would sell out in about half the time.
+Sellers already feel it. More than half of the homes for sale have cut their asking price. About 4 in 10 houses and more than half of condos for sale have been listed more than 90 days.
+Photo: Tom Long, CC BY-SA 2.0
+The beach beside the SkyWheel in downtown Myrtle Beach, inside city limits.
 
-Constrained supply. Coastal land is finite and building has not kept pace with arrivals. More buyers than homes pushes prices up.
+House prices have stopped climbing. A federal index of Horry County house prices rose about 51 percent in four years, then was about flat in the latest year. It tracks houses, not condos.
+Sales follow the season. Recorded resales were fewest in January and February, about 430 houses a month, and were about 620 to 670 a month from May to October.
 
-Affordability. Named the 4th most affordable beach town in America by House Beautiful, so out-of-state buyers arriving with home-sale equity stretch further here and keep demand high.
+## Where in Myrtle Beach do buyers have the most room to negotiate?
+Condos in the City of Myrtle Beach have the most supply of any town here. Houses in Surfside Beach, Little River, Carolina Forest, Forestbrook and Red Hill have under 4 months.
+Many buyers say "Myrtle Beach" for the whole coast. The map and the table split it into ten towns and communities, from Little River to Garden City and inland to Conway.
+Towns shaded by months of houses for sale, with the chart's colors. The outlines are the Census Bureau's town and community lines. Map data: U.S. Census Bureau TIGER/Line; Horry County GIS.
 
-Unique SC tax breaks. South Carolina does not tax Social Security income, taxes a primary home at a 4% assessment ratio, fully exempts 100% disabled veterans from property tax on their home, and gives owners 65 and older a Homestead exemption on the first $50,000 of value (SC Department of Revenue).
+- 1 Myrtle Beach
 
-Rental income. A year-round tourism economy lets many homes earn short-term-rental income, adding investor demand on top of buyers.
