" paint-order="stroke">mid-2021</text><circle cx="590.0" cy="39.6" r="4.5" fill="#1c2028"/><text x="586.0" y="27.6" text-anchor="end" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028" stroke="#fbf8f3" stroke-width="4" stroke-linejoin="round" paint-order="stroke">now: up 52%</text></svg><figcaption style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.6;margin-top:.6rem">Change in house prices in Horry County since mid‑2021, by quarter: up about half by mid‑2025, then about flat for a year. Federal Housing Finance Agency house price index; houses only.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="online-estimates"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Online estimates">How accurate are online home estimates in Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Redfin publishes how close its own estimate comes in Horry County. For homes that were not for sale, the middle miss was 6.6 percent: about $23,000 on a $350,000 house. Half of the homes were further off, and about 1 in 7 was off by more than 20 percent.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No online estimate, Zillow's Zestimate included, tells you whether your home is one of the close ones or one of the far ones.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Once a home is listed for sale, Redfin's middle miss here falls to 1.65 percent. It is closer then because there is more data about a home for sale. A listing adds what the county's records do not show: the rooms, the updates, the photos and an asking price. Our agent looks at those things with you before you list, and sends the number with the sales behind it.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="county-value"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Quick numbers">Is the county's value or the nearest sales a good guess at my price?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Two quick numbers are often used as a price: the middle price of the six nearest sales, and the county's value, which is the market value on the home's county property record. We checked both against the prices of houses sold this year.</p><figure class="c3-gchart"><div role="table" aria-label="How far off two quick numbers were on a typical house"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">How far off two quick numbers were on a typical house</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Quick number</span><span role="columnheader">how far off</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Middle price of the six nearest sales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.805" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">16% off</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">The county's value</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.44" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">9% off</span></span></div></div></div></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The county's value was closer, but it is a mass estimate, made for every home at once, for tax. It is above the price on most condos and below it on most houses: about 7 in 10 condos sold for less than it, and 6 in 10 houses sold for more.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Your tax bill is a worse guide. About 2 in 3 Horry County homes are taxed on less than the county's value. A sale resets the value as of December 31 of the year the home sold, so <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">your buyer's tax bill</a> can be higher than yours.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="how-we-price-it"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="How we price it">How is a home's value worked out in Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We start from the sales most like your home. The nearest sales, or the sales on your street, can be far off. Here is one Carolina Forest house from the county's records.</p><figure style="margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:760px"><svg class="hv-n" viewBox="0 0 300 218" role="img" aria-label="Dot chart on one price line from $250,000 to $750,000. The six nearest sales are dots from $270,000 to $720,000, with their middle at $556,000. The other sales in the house's own neighborhood are dots from $356,000 to $425,000. The house sold for $482,000, more than any of them."><line x1="39.6" x2="39.6" y1="21.7" y2="214" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".08"/><text x="39.6" y="15.5" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15.5" fill="#4a4f5c">$300k</text><line x1="150.0" x2="150.0" y1="21.7" y2="214" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".08"/><text x="150.0" y="15.5" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15.5" fill="#4a4f5c">$500k</text><line x1="260.4" x2="260.4" y1="21.7" y2="214" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".08"/><text x="260.4" y="15.5" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15.5" fill="#4a4f5c">$700k</text><line x1="140.1" x2="140.1" y1="47.1" y2="69.1" stroke="#c4783a" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><line x1="140.1" x2="140.1" y1="137.8" y2="159.8" stroke="#c4783a" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><line x1="140.1" x2="140.1" y1="189.7" y2="211.7" stroke="#c4783a" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><text x="12" y="40.3" font-size="15.5" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028" stroke="#fbf8f3" stroke-width="5" stroke-linejoin="round" paint-order="stroke">The six nearest 