+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Is anything held back if I sell at a loss?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not if you sign Form I‑295. The hold-back is figured on your gain, and a home sold for less than it cost you has no gain. Without the form, it is figured on the price less your selling costs."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does the notary have to be from South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. A notary in the state where you live can notarize the deed, and the act counts the same as a South Carolina notary's. The notary writes in their state and the date their commission ends."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I pay unpaid HOA dues at closing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Usually, yes. Unpaid dues are put as a lien on the home and taken out of the seller's money at closing. Our agents check for them before closing, so they do not surprise you."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is fall a bad time to sell to out-of-state buyers?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. More buyers from other states closed in October than in any other month. Even in January, the slowest month, about 1 in 4 buyers came from another state."}}]}</script>
